  "notes": [
    "Every GPIO is 3.3 V and none are 5 V tolerant.",
    "Peripheral mappings are not fixed: each pin lists the SPI/I2C/UART instance it can reach, and you pick one in software. Two pins on the same instance cannot be used for the same role at once.",
    "GPIO23, 24, 25 and 29 exist on the RP2040 but are not on the header, they drive the SMPS mode, VBUS sense, the on-board LED and VSYS sense. On the Pico W they are taken over by the wireless chip, which is why the LED moves to the CYW43 on that model.",
    "VSYS (pin 39) accepts 1.8-5.5 V and feeds the on-board buck-boost regulator. VBUS (pin 40) is USB 5 V directly.",
    "ADC_VREF (pin 35) is a filtered 3.3 V reference; tie analog grounds to AGND (pin 33), not a digital GND pin, for quieter readings.",
    "The Pico 2 uses the same header and the same GP numbers on an RP2350: more RAM, a choice of Arm or RISC-V cores, and 5 V tolerant digital pins."
  ],
